          "name": "Conciergerie",
          "map": "Conciergerie Paris",
          "text": "Next door, the Conciergerie was a medieval royal palace before it became the Revolution's most feared prison. Marie-Antoinette spent her final weeks in a cell here before the guillotine in 1793; a small chapel now marks the spot. Downstairs, don't miss the cavernous Hall of the Men-at-Arms, one of the largest surviving medieval halls in Europe."
        },
        {
          "name": "Pont Neuf & Square du Vert-Galant",
          "map": "Square du Vert-Galant Paris",
          "text": "Finish on the Pont Neuf — the 'New Bridge' that is, ironically, Paris's oldest (1607), and the first built without houses so people could stroll and watch the river. Look for the rows of grotesque carved faces (mascarons) along its sides. Then take the steps down to the Square du Vert-Galant, the leafy little park at the island's very point — a local picnic spot and a perfect place to end, with the Seine sliding past on both sides."
        }
